    "title": "If you rented from one of America’s largest landlords you might get money back for hidden fees",
    "text": "NewsPublished: Mar. 14, 2026, 6:00 a.m.WASHINGTON, DC - AUGUST 20: A sign masks the location of the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) headquarters building on August 20, 2025, in Washington, DC. (Photo by J. David Ake/Getty Images)Getty ImagesMore than $47.2 million will be paid to 444,131 consumers affected by a government lawsuit against one of the largest single-family home landlords, the Federal Trade Commission announced.The settlement is a part of a lawsuit filed by the FTC in September 2024 accusing Invitation Home of “an array of unlawful actions” against consumers. In the lawsuit, the FTC alleged that the company engaged in deceptive practices, including misleading applicants about lease costs, charging renters undisclosed fees, not inspecting homes before tenants moved in, and unfairly withholding tenants’ security deposits when they moved out. The undisclosed fees that renters could not opt out of services such as “smart home technology” and “utility management,” which added $60 per month to tenants rental payments. Another fee included an internet package that cost $85 per month. The FTC also accused the company of unfair and deceptive charges when renters moved out, such as wear-and-tear charges, damages that happened before renters moved in and renovations.Invitation Homes agreed to pay $48 million total with $47.2 million going to consumers. The company was required to clearly disclose its leasing prices, establish policies and procedures related to fairly distributing security deposit refunds and to stop any unlawful behavior. RECOMMENDEDThe company did not admit or deny any wrongdoing and waived its rights to appeal or challenge the lawsuit, according to court documents. Consumers eligible for compensation must have paid Invitation Homes $45 or more for fees or charges from January 2021 through September 2024. Anyone who has already received a credit or a refund is not eligible for a settlement payment. Those who have questions about their payment should contact Rust Consulting Inc., the refund administrator, at 800-804-6915 or by email at info@InvitationHomesRefund.com.",
